\title{hypertabular: Forward-Only Reads of Delimited Text and Workbooks, Every Cell a Verdict}
\description{
Reads delimited text and 'XLSX', 'ODS' and 'Numbers' workbooks a batch at a time into typed columns, under a dialect, a plan of columns and the formats, field orders, precisions and epochs the caller declares rather than guesses. Every cell is judged by the 'hypercast' package's casts and comes back as its verdict: the value, or a closed reason (empty, malformed, out of range) with the offending span of the cell's text. A file that is structurally broken comes back as a failure value after every intact row before it. The work is done by a compiled 'Rust' core shared with the project's bindings for other languages, which neither allocates nor panics; no read signals an error for its data.
}
\details{
Readers for delimited text (\code{\link[=delimited_reader]{delimited_reader()}}, \code{\link[=read_delimited]{read_delimited()}}) and for XLSX, ODS
and Numbers workbooks (\code{\link[=workbook]{workbook()}}, \code{\link[=sheet_reader]{sheet_reader()}}, \code{\link[=read_sheet]{read_sheet()}}), through a plan of
columns that each name a source column and the HyperCast door to cast it with
(\code{\link[=col_bool]{col_bool()}} and its siblings). Every cell comes back as a HyperCast verdict, in the
\code{hypercast_verdict} data frames the hypercast package's own doors return: the value, or a
fault with its reason and the offending span of the cell's text. A file that is
structurally broken comes back as a \link{hypertabular_failure} value, after every intact row
before it. No read signals an R error for anything in its data; errors are reserved for
caller bugs in what declares how to read it (a dialect, a plan, a sheet), as in every
other HyperTabular binding.
